# Copy to the file named in each section's heading below and fill in real values.
# `.env*.local` is gitignored; never commit real credentials.
#
# For LOCAL DEVELOPMENT specifically: copy this file's runtime section to apps/web/.env.local, not
# to a repo-root .env.local. `next dev` runs with cwd apps/web and reads .env*.local only from its
# own project directory — it does not walk up to a monorepo root — so a repo-root .env.local (the
# one `vercel env pull` writes) is invisible to `pnpm dev` and DATABASE_URL reads as unset.
# See docs/decisions/0054-local-development-writes-to-the-preview-database.md.

# --- Application runtime (apps/web/.env.local for local dev; repo-root .env.local is what
# --- `vercel env pull` manages for Vercel's own Development environment) ---

# Postgres on a standalone Neon project (not the Vercel Marketplace integration —
# see docs/decisions/0007-standalone-neon-instead-of-vercel-managed.md).
# Use the POOLED connection string (hostname contains "-pooler"), and the least-privilege
# `app_designs` role — NOT the database owner. That role holds SELECT/INSERT/UPDATE on `designs`
# and nothing else, so it cannot drop, truncate or delete. See
# docs/decisions/0008-least-privilege-database-role.md.
#
# Which database this points at depends on which file it is in:
#   - repo-root .env.local (`vercel env pull`, Vercel's Development environment) -> lemn-conf-v2
#     (PRODUCTION). Do not repoint pnpm dev at this file — see the note above.
#   - apps/web/.env.local (local development, see docs/decisions/0054) -> lemn-conf-v2-preview.
#     The same database Vercel preview deploys write to; see
#     docs/decisions/0009-deployment-configuration-gaps.md's 2026-09-02 amendment.
DATABASE_URL=

# Vercel Blob store holding the scheduled design-table exports (private access).
BLOB_READ_WRITE_TOKEN=

# Shared secret Vercel Cron sends as `Authorization: Bearer <value>` to the backup endpoint.
CRON_SECRET=

# The key every keyed digest is made under: the rate limiter's buckets, and a contact request's payload. Thirty-two
# random bytes written as sixty-four lowercase hexadecimal characters; make it yourself, once for each environment, with
# `openssl rand -hex 32`, and use a different value in each. Never print it, log it or put it in a browser bundle.
# Left commented so that copying this file defines nothing. While it is unset or unusable, no design can be created.
# DIGEST_SECRET=

# Opt-in for the internal capture route `/internal/scene-shot`, which is verification tooling and
# not a customer surface. Unset (or any other value) means a production build serves 404 there;
# set it to exactly `1` only in an environment where the fidelity capture harness must run.
# `apps/web/scripts/capture-fidelity.mjs` sets it on the server it spawns, so local capture needs
# nothing here. Leave it UNSET in the deployed application.
# LEMNARIA_ENABLE_SCENE_SHOT_ROUTE=

# Opt-in for the `SelectionDebug` readout, the hidden span the Playwright suite reads selection and
# save state from. It is test instrumentation, not a customer surface, so it does not render in any
# build that does not set this to exactly `1` — including ordinary `next dev`, deliberately:
# development is not the e2e suite. `apps/web/playwright.config.ts` sets it on the server it starts,
# so running the suite needs nothing here. Leave it UNSET in the deployed application.
# The `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix is required — the reader is a client component and Next inlines only
# that prefix into the browser bundle.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LEMNARIA_ENABLE_SELECTION_DEBUG=

# --- Mail: the shop's notification of a contact request, sent through Google (Gmail) SMTP by nodemailer.
# --- The three live names are read on the server, when a request is to be notified, and are left commented so that
# --- copying this file defines none of them. A deployment that sets some but not all of them, or one that is
# --- unusable, cannot send, and says so. Set them on Vercel (sensitive). Nothing local needs them: a local copy is
# --- optional and goes only in the repo-root .env.local, never apps/web/.env.local, whose mail names the local
# --- server loads and which makes the e2e contact specs fail by design.

# The sending Google account: exactly one plain address, on an account with 2-Step Verification turned on. The notice
# leaves from it, and a dedicated account is recommended over the shop's own inbox (the default until the owner
# decides).
# GOOGLE_EMAIL=

# That account's App password (Google Account > Security > 2-Step Verification > App passwords). Paste it without
# spaces; a value with a space is refused as unusable. A secret: never print it, log it, or put it in a browser bundle.
# It opens the whole mailbox, not only sending.
# GOOGLE_APP_PASSWORD=

# The shop's inbox: the only recipient of any message. Exactly one address, never a customer's.
# LEAD_NOTIFICATION_ADDRESS=

# RETIRED, no longer read for configuration: the previous provider's names. They are kept here, and kept in any
# environment that already holds them, because variables are add-only. The code ignores them for sending, but the
# e2e fake's flag below still refuses to take effect beside either one.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=

# Opt-in for the recording stand-in that replaces the mail transport under the e2e suite: it sends nothing and
# answers every request as accepted. It takes effect only when set to exactly `1` and none of the five names above
# (the three live and the two retired) is set; beside any of them the server treats mail as unavailable.
# Test instrumentation, not a customer surface. Leave it UNSET in the deployed application.
# LEMNARIA_E2E_FAKE_MAIL=

# Names whose network address the rate limiter trusts. Set it to exactly `vercel` on a deployment on that platform
# only, which overwrites the `X-Forwarded-For` header with the client's real address. Never set it in a local file,
# on any other host, or for the platform's own development target. Left unset, every request is counted in one
# shared bucket; set to anything else, every attempt to create a design is refused. Do not read it from a variable
# the platform sets itself.
# CLIENT_NETWORK_SOURCE=

# Opt-in for the e2e suite's rate-limit policy: a day's figure of 1,100 designs for the one shared bucket, in place of
# 20 for each network block. Test instrumentation, not a customer surface: `apps/web/playwright.config.ts` sets it,
# with a secret of its own, on the server it starts, so running the suite needs nothing here. It takes effect only
# when set to exactly `1` and `CLIENT_NETWORK_SOURCE` is not set; beside that variable every attempt to create a
# design is refused. Leave it UNSET on every deployment.
# LEMNARIA_E2E_RATE_LIMIT_POLICY=

# The site's public origin, which the server writes the shop's view-only kitchen link under: exactly `https://` and
# the host, with an optional port and nothing after it (no path, no trailing `/`, lower case, an IDN in punycode).
# A loopback host (localhost, 127.0.0.1, [::1]), over `http://` or `https://`, is accepted only where the e2e fake
# sender's flag above is also set to 1, so a local `next dev` without it sends its notice with the link unavailable.
# One value per environment, matching the database that environment uses. Not a secret. Left commented so that copying
# this file defines nothing; while it is unset or unusable, the notice is still sent, without the link.
# LEMNARIA_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=

# --- Migrations only (.env.migration.local, NOT .env.local) ---
# The owner-role connection. Migrations need DDL rights that DATABASE_URL's role deliberately
# lacks, which is why this is a separate variable rather than a fallback: the running application
# must never hold credentials that can drop a table. Used by `pnpm --filter
# @lemnaria/persistence run migrate <environment>`.
#
# The <environment> argument is required and is checked, not just printed: both `migrate` and
# `migrate:status` refuse to run if the declared environment doesn't match the database this
# connection string actually reaches (`packages/persistence/src/migration-target.ts`; see
# docs/milestones/migration-environment-safety-check.md). `production` and `preview` are checked
# against their own database names; an unlisted label (e.g. a one-off restore-drill database) is
# accepted only if it equals the database name exactly.
#
# Points at lemn-conf-v2 (production) under the lemn-conf-v2-boss owner role. To migrate a
# different database (e.g. lemn-conf-v2-preview after a schema change — see
# docs/decisions/0009-deployment-configuration-gaps.md's 2026-09-02 amendment, which records that
# preview is not migrated by any automated process today), pass its owner connection string
# explicitly rather than repointing this variable, and declare the matching environment name.
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Tests only (.env.test.local, NOT .env.local) ---
# A database live tests may write to and delete from. Must not be the application database:
# resolveTestDatabaseUrl() refuses to run if it resolves to the same database as DATABASE_URL.
# See docs/decisions/0006-tests-never-touch-the-application-database.md.
#
# Points at lemn-conf-v2-staging under the staging-boss owner role. Despite the name this is NOT a
# staging environment and nothing but the live persistence test suites may use it — every run
# deletes design rows it minted, and separately alters the `designs` table's own schema (columns
# dropped and re-added, `schema_migrations` dropped) as part of testing migrations. See
# docs/decisions/0006-tests-never-touch-the-application-database.md's 2026-08-31 amendment before
# pointing anything else at this database.
# TEST_DATABASE_URL=

# --- Reference only (.env.prod.local) ---
# Not read by any script. A human-maintained record of the owner-role connection strings for
# production (lemn-conf-v2, OWNER_DATABASE_URL/APP_DATABASE_URL) and preview
# (lemn-conf-v2-preview, PREVIEW_OWNER_DATABASE_URL/PREVIEW_DATABASE_URL), kept alongside the
# app_designs application role for each. Consult it rather than re-deriving these values by hand.
